==================================================================
DAY 17 - PART A: THEORY
DNS in Kubernetes (CoreDNS)
Time: 30-40 minutes
==================================================================

1. WHO PROVIDES DNS?
--------------------
CoreDNS runs as a Deployment in kube-system, behind a Service called
"kube-dns" (ClusterIP usually 10.96.0.10). The kubelet writes that IP
into every pod's /etc/resolv.conf.

CoreDNS watches the API server for Services and Pods and answers DNS
questions about them. Its config is a ConfigMap called "coredns"
(the Corefile).


2. SERVICE DNS NAMES
--------------------
For Service "backend" in namespace "shop":

  backend.shop.svc.cluster.local
     |      |   |      |
  service  ns  fixed  cluster domain

Records:
  A/AAAA   -> the ClusterIP (normal Service)
  A/AAAA   -> each pod IP  (headless Service)
  SRV      -> _http._tcp.backend.shop.svc.cluster.local (named ports)

StatefulSet pods behind a headless Service also get their own names:
  web-0.web.shop.svc.cluster.local  (Week 4)


3. SEARCH DOMAINS: WHY "backend" WORKS
--------------------------------------
A pod in namespace "shop" has /etc/resolv.conf like:

  nameserver 10.96.0.10
  search shop.svc.cluster.local svc.cluster.local cluster.local
  options ndots:5

When you look up "backend", the resolver tries:
  backend.shop.svc.cluster.local      <- found
So:
  "backend"        works within the same namespace
  "backend.shop"   works from any namespace
  full name        always works


4. ndots:5 AND EXTERNAL NAMES
-----------------------------
A name with fewer than 5 dots is tried with each search domain FIRST.
"api.github.com" (2 dots) causes several failed lookups before the
real one. That's extra latency. Fixes: use a trailing dot
("api.github.com.") or lower ndots in pod dnsConfig.


5. EXTERNAL NAMES
-----------------
Names CoreDNS doesn't own are forwarded to the node's upstream DNS
(the "forward . /etc/resolv.conf" line in the Corefile).


6. dnsPolicy
------------
  ClusterFirst (default)   use CoreDNS
  Default                  use the node's DNS settings
  None                     use only what you set in dnsConfig
  ClusterFirstWithHostNet  for hostNetwork pods


7. WHEN DNS BREAKS
------------------
Symptoms: "could not resolve host", slow first requests.
Check in this order:
  1. Is CoreDNS running?   k get pods -n kube-system -l k8s-app=kube-dns
  2. Does the Service exist, in which namespace?
  3. From a pod: nslookup name; cat /etc/resolv.conf
  4. CoreDNS logs:         k logs -n kube-system -l k8s-app=kube-dns


KEY POINTS TO REMEMBER
----------------------
- <service>.<namespace>.svc.cluster.local
- Search domains let you use short names.
- CoreDNS config = ConfigMap "coredns" in kube-system.
